00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:47.240
the rest stays put. Most of the schedule, he says, is verification and the loops that chase

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.760
performance, power, and area. The offset is that checking C is orders of magnitude faster

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.660
than checking RTL, and HLS can synthesize quickly, so an agent could try many alternatives

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:04.740
in a short time. He also says that coupling is not here yet. Diptash non-dead microchip draws

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:10.980
the line in power, not in lines of code. The goal is not to push every block through HLS and

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:17.520
not to have a language model emit all of the RTL. Some portion exists for time to market. Use HLS,

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:23.340
he says, and it will probably burn more power, because it will not be optimized the way hand-crafted

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:29.460
RTL is. His FPGA picture of the flow is a path into a device, not a claim that every

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:35.500
block belongs on it. Tom Demure at Keysight EDA orders the stack the same way. Software

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:41.800
leads, digital design comes next, then mixed analog, then full physics, and high level synthesis

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:47.340
sits in the digital part behind software. His limit on prompting is specific. You cannot

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:52.240
hand a vague prompt to this industry and expect a production-ready chip because a tape out

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.720
needs context of vague sentence does not carry.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:59.580
Trust is the older constraint, and it is why the short description does not retire the

00:09:59.580 --> 00:09:59.980
